  ...
  _Future<T> _future = _Future<T>();
  Timer? timer;
  ...
  this.then((T v) {
    if (timer != null) timer.cancel();
    _future._completeWithValue(v);
  }, onError: (Object e, StackTrace s) {
    if (timer != null) timer.cancel();
    _future._completeError(e, s);
  });
  timer = Timer(timeLimit, () {
    ...
    if (onTimeout == null) _future._completeError(TimeoutException...);
    else { result = onTimeout(); ... _future._completeWithValue/complete(result) }
  });
  return _future;
}
```

Note: `this.then(...)` — the source future has a listener registered by `.timeout`. Even after the timer completes the wrapper future, the source's then-callbacks remain registered (there's no cancel — `then` on a future cannot be cancelled). So when the source (fetchLatestTag's future) later completes with an error (because we closed the client under it), the `onError` callback in `.timeout` RUNS and calls `_future._completeError(e, s)` — but the wrapper `_future` already completed with the timeout value. Completing an already-completed future with an error → in the implementation, `_future._completeError` on an already-completed `_Future` is a no-op? Actually `_Future._completeError` asserts !_isComplete in debug mode but in production it... hmm. Let me recall: dart async `_Future._completeError` calls `_completeError(error, stackTrace)` → `_asyncCompleteError` — if the future is already complete, this throws a StateError? I remember: "Future._completeError on an already-completed future" — in the SDK, `_Future._completeError` has `assert(!_isComplete)` — an assert only active in debug/test mode! In production (AOT), asserts are off, and completing an already-complete future is silently ignored... Actually the implementation:

```dart
void _completeError(Object error, StackTrace stackTrace) {
  assert(!_isComplete);
  ...
}
```

Hmm wait, actually in newer SDK versions `_asyncCompleteError` checks. Let me think about what actually happens in practice: calling `completer.completeError` twice throws StateError ("Future already completed") — but that's for Completer. `_Future._completeError` (internal) — the assert is only an assert. In release mode, an already-completed future ignores... no — it would corrupt state or throw?

This matters for the common case: blackholed network (timeout fires at 3s) → we close the client → the in-flight request errors with ClientException → `.timeout`'s internal onError tries to complete the already-completed wrapper future. In the Dart SDK (2.19+/3.x), `Future.timeout` is implemented via `FutureExtensions.timeout` → it creates `_Future` and calls `this.then(..., onError: ...)` where onError does `_future._completeError(e, s)` guarded... let me actually check the SDK source in the pub cache or dart SDK on this machine. If the guard is only an assert, tests on the VM (which run with asserts ENABLED by default for the SDK's own dart:async? No — SDK internal asserts are compiled out in the shipped SDK... actually dart:async ships as part of the core snapshot; its asserts are NOT enabled in normal runs).

Rather than speculating deeply: the round-2 fix pattern (close client while request in flight) creates exactly this double-complete scenario whenever the timeout fires while the request is still pending. The author claims they ran the engine... they couldn't run dart local…

